@page "/downloads"
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@using Portal.Channels
@inject Catalogue Catalogue
@inject IOptions<PortalOptions> Options
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Downloads";
    // pkg comes from the channel that carries it: "pkg" unless pinned elsewhere.
    var pkgHome = Catalogue.Pinned().FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "pkg")?.Channel ?? "pkg";
    var ch = Catalogue.Get(pkgHome);
    var pkg = ch?.Packages.GetValueOrDefault("pkg");
}
<h1 class="sr-only">Downloads</h1>
@if (ch is null || pkg is null)
{
    <p class="muted">pkg is not published yet.</p>
    return;
}
@{
    var dir = System.IO.Path.Combine(Options.Value.ChannelsDir, ch.Name);
    var site = Options.Value.PublicUrl.Length > 0 ? Options.Value.PublicUrl.TrimEnd('/') : $"{Request.Scheme}://{Request.Host}";
    var plain = "http://" + site[(site.IndexOf("://", StringComparison.Ordinal) + 3)..];
    var cpus = Bootstrap.ArosCpus(dir);
    var hosts = ChannelPaths.HostBootstraps.Where(h => System.IO.File.Exists(System.IO.Path.Combine(dir, h.Value))).ToList();
    var sh = $"curl -fsSL {site}/install | sh";
    var ps = $"irm {site}/install.ps1 | iex";
    var getpkg = $"wget -q -O RAM:Get-Pkg {plain}/Get-Pkg\nExecute RAM:Get-Pkg";
    var upgrade = $"Pkg UPGRADE ALL ROOT SYS: CHANNEL {site}/{ch.Name}";
    static string HostName(string platform) => platform switch
    {
        "macos-arm64" => "macOS, Apple Silicon", "macos-x86_64" => "macOS, Intel",
        "linux-x86_64" => "Linux x86_64", "linux-arm64" => "Linux aarch64", _ => "Windows",
    };
}
<p class="lead">pkg @pkg.Latest.Version, the one program that installs the rest. Let it install itself with one line; it lands as a signed package, and <code>Pkg UPGRADE pkg</code> keeps it current from then on.</p>


<div class="grid downloads-grid">
<div>
    <h2 class="label">On AROS</h2>
    <p>With a network and <code>wget</code> (AROS One, Icaros, the nightly with its contrib), in a Shell:</p>
    <div class="cmd lines"><code>@getpkg</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@getpkg">Copy</button></div>
    <p class="muted small">It fetches the pkg for this machine's CPU, which then installs the signed <code>pkg</code> package from the portal. If <code>wget</code> ends at once without a word, it wants its settings file first: <code>Echo "" &gt;ENV:wgetcfg</code>. Without a network, take the drawer for your CPU on the right, copy it over, and run <code>Execute &lt;drawer&gt;/Install-Pkg &lt;drawer&gt;</code>.</p>

    <h2 class="label">On macOS and Linux</h2>
    <p>In a terminal, to manage a hosted AROS, a disk image or a drawer from your computer:</p>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@sh</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@sh">Copy</button></div>
    <p class="muted small">It picks the build for your computer, checks it against the portal's signed checksums, and puts <code>pkg</code> where you can run it by name. On a Mac it also drops the AROS drawer into the folder a hosted AROS shares. Run it again to upgrade. Read it first: <a href="/install.sh">install.sh</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="label">On Windows</h2>
    <p>In PowerShell:</p>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@ps</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@ps">Copy</button></div>
    <p class="muted small">The same checks, into <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\pkg</code>. Read it first: <a href="/install.ps1">install.ps1</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="label">Then</h2>
    <div class="cmd"><code>@upgrade</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@upgrade">Copy</button></div>
    <p class="muted">keeps everything you installed current, pkg included. Taking something out again: <a href="/docs/removing">Removing software</a>. Where packages come from: <a href="/channels">Channels</a>.</p>
</div>
<aside class="rail">
    <div class="card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Every file, for a machine that cannot fetch its own</span>
        <span class="card-title">Download pkg @pkg.Latest.Version</span>
        <span class="muted small">This is the way we like least: a file you download installs nothing by itself, and you check it yourself against the <a href="/trust">signed checksums</a>. The lines on the left do both for you.</span>
        <ul class="files">
        @foreach (var cpu in cpus)
        {
            var f = Bootstrap.Offer(dir, $"Bootstrap/{cpu}/Pkg");
            <li><a href="/get/@ch.Name/pkg-@(cpu).zip">AROS @cpu</a><span class="muted">drawer · @Site.Size(f.Size)</span></li>
        }
        @foreach (var (platform, rel) in hosts)
        {
            var f = Bootstrap.Offer(dir, rel);
            <li><a href="/get/@ch.Name/@platform">@HostName(platform)</a><span class="muted">@Site.Size(f.Size)</span></li>
        }
        </ul>
        <span class="muted small">An AROS drawer is a channel of its own: unpack it and run <code>Execute &lt;drawer&gt;/Install-Pkg &lt;drawer&gt;</code>, which installs the signed package through pkg's own checks.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <span class="eyebrow">Check what you downloaded</span>
        <span class="muted small">Every file here is listed with its SHA-256 in <code>Bootstrap/SHA256SUMS</code>, signed by the portal's bootstrap key. The installers check that signature before they install anything; by hand, <a href="/trust">Trust</a> shows the commands.</span>
    </div>
    <section class="card embed-card" aria-labelledby="embed-pkg">
        <h2 id="embed-pkg" class="eyebrow">Update checks in your own application</h2>
        <p class="small">Embed <code>libpkg</code> to check for a newer compatible build and display signed release notes. Configure the package, root and channel in code or a <code>.pkgupdate</code> file. Your application asks the user before requesting an upgrade.</p>
        <p class="small"><a href="/docs/self-update">Read the integration guide</a>, with a C example and a local HTTP demo.</p>
    </section>
</aside>
</div>
